by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-02T08:15:17Z

Thanks Amoifr, the DI wiring and the batch handling are right now. Rebased on 8.2, with four changes before merging.

The `$secret` argument was accepted and never used, so any well-formed JSON posted to the endpoint was dispatched onto the bus and a configured secret did nothing. Event Grid does not sign its payloads, which is why there was nothing obvious to verify, but it does authenticate itself another way: it repeats the query parameters of the subscription URL on every delivery, and Microsoft documents that as the way to secure a webhook that is not behind Entra ID. So the parser now compares a `secret` query parameter against the configured secret with `hash_equals()` and rejects a mismatch with a 401. The subscription URL becomes `https://example.com/webhook/azure?secret=...`, and the README says so.

`Hardening tests` was failing: the repo requires every `AbstractRequestParser` to ship a test that expects a `RejectWebhookException`, and the allow-list is closed. `AzureMalformedPayloadRequestParserTest` covers the seven rejection paths, so the 401 and 406 branches are no longer untested.

The `isset()` guards checked presence, not type, so a payload with an array where a string belongs still reached the setters and produced a 500 rather than a 406. They check the type now, and one of the new rejection cases pins it.

Two fixtures were added, `expanded` and `suppressed`. Five of the seven documented statuses had none, so the mapping was free to drift: flipping `Expanded` to `DROPPED`, or the `Suppressed`/`Quarantined`/`FilteredSpam`/`Failed` group to `BOUNCE`, both left the suite green. Both mutations now fail.

The description was rewritten to read as documentation and ends with the points a symfony-docs PR should carry.
